course: dictionary
lesson: 2309
title: Two meanings, one word
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Context, the words around the word, tells you which meaning the writer wants. Look at the clues first. «Frogs, toads, newts and salamanders are amphibians; snakes, lizards, crocodiles and turtles are reptiles.» The list of animals is the clue: crocodile is an animal here.
explain: Now try a different meaning in the same place. In the UK, a class might walk «in a crocodile» on a school trip. A long line of children cannot be a big reptile with sharp teeth, so this sentence must mean a line of children walking in pairs. Always ask: does my meaning make sense in the sentence?
explain: The first meaning fits a sentence like «Each year more than a million wildebeest... even across crocodile rivers.» Here the rivers are dangerous because crocodiles live in them. When a word has two meanings, the clues in the sentence decide which one is right.
fact: Crocodile has two meanings: a large reptile, and (UK) a long line of children walking in pairs.
fact: A crocodile is a reptile with a long body, thick scaly skin, many sharp teeth and a powerful tail.
fact: Crocodiles live in rivers and swamps in hot countries, and they are cold-blooded.
fact: In Australia, saltwater crocodiles are a real danger: obey warning signs and never swim where crocs live.
fact: Caimans are cousins of crocodiles, and they live in the Pantanal in Brazil.
exercise: In «Each year more than a million wildebeest... even across crocodile rivers», what is a crocodile? | A children's line | *A large reptile that lives in rivers | A type of bird | A small fish
exercise: A teacher says, «Walk in a crocodile, please, class.» What does crocodile mean here? | A big tropical storm | A large reptile with sharp teeth | A hot swamp | *A long line of children walking in pairs
exercise: Find and copy the word that names the group crocodiles belong to: «snakes, lizards, crocodiles and turtles are reptiles» | *reptiles | turtles | amphibians | snakes
test: What does «At last the Crocodile let go of the Elephant's Child's nose with a plop that you could hear all up and down the Limpopo» tell you about the crocodile? | *It is a large animal with a strong grip, in a river | It is a bird that sings | It is a line of children | It is a shop
test: What does «caimans (cousins of crocodiles)» tell you? | Caimans are a kind of children's line | Caimans live only in deserts | Caimans are rivers | *Caimans are closely related to crocodiles
exam: Jayden says, «The class went out in a crocodile.» Which meaning fits, and how do you know? | A reptile, because it has teeth | *A line of children walking in pairs, because a class walking out fits this | A swamp, because classes go to swamps | A river, because it is wet
